  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14192941/assets/f1d9c516171eade5488c3247bd20d0bfcafd515e4da7de8aee3fe35174a87e8b.png)\n\nOn which interval is the function $y=f(x)$, shown above, strictly increasing?",
  "worked_solution": "If we follow the graph from left to right, we can see that: \n\n- For $x\\in (-\\infty ,-2)$ the graph falls\n\n- For $x\\in (-2,0)$ the graph is flat\n\n- For $x\\in (0,2)$ the graph rises\n\n- For $x\\in (2,\\infty )$ the graph falls\n\nThe function is strictly increasing on the intervals where it always rises: $(0,2)$ only.",
